    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks, that helps a lot. To wrap this up, I wanted to touch on the unpredictability of the reaction in a real-world kitchen setting. Sometimes, even when you follow the pH adjustments or temperature controls perfectly, the browning doesn't happen uniformly, or it seems to \"misbehave\"—like getting bitter spots or uneven coloring in ways the math doesn't easily predict. From a chemical standpoint, what are the main factors that cause this kind of inconsistency during the transition to melanoidin formation?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The unpredictability in a real-world kitchen setting is often due to the sheer number of competing side reactions and the heterogeneous nature of the food matrix. Even if you control the bulk pH and temperature, localized variations can occur, which is where the chaos begins.\n\nOne major factor is the presence of trace contaminants or inhibitors within the substrate itself, which can act as localized catalysts or, conversely, slow down the reaction in specific pockets. Furthermore, the formation of highly reactive, short-lived intermediates—like reactive carbonyl species—is extremely sensitive to local concentration gradients. These intermediates can react with other molecules, leading to polymerization or the formation of bitter-tasting compounds before they can successfully integrate into the desired melanoidin structure.",
      "source": "target",
